From 9b887cfccef5b9c97ff0c3853d107b2fc6230eaa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alex Gleason <alex@alexgleason.me>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 18:40:08 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] :bug: Collapse guest RAM into huge pages only where asked

On a Radeon 890M host (RADV, Mesa 26.1.8, Linux 6.18.59), a macOS 26.7.2
guest on reims-graphics-pci over collapsed RAM panicked 80 to 154 s into
every boot, its kernel's memory overwritten: pmap's pv lists, an rw lock,
a ledger, IOKit matching. With the same memfd preallocated and the
collapse skipped, it ran clean for 11 minutes, then 9 more at 8 vCPUs
and 12 GiB, with apps opening throughout. With plain VGA in the device's
place, collapsed RAM ran clean too: the panics need both.

Skip the collapse unless QEMU_HOSTMEM_COLLAPSE=1, until where it goes wrong
(the import of udmabufs over large shmem folios, or KVM's 2 MiB maps beside
it) is found. reims-vgpu#185 has the bisect.
---
 backends/hostmem.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/backends/hostmem.c b/backends/hostmem.c
index d5fa574..61f6850 100644
--- a/backends/hostmem.c
+++ b/backends/hostmem.c
@@ -364,6 +364,15 @@ static void host_memory_backend_collapse(HostMemoryBackend *backend,
     int64_t t0;
     int err = 0;
 
+    /*
+     * Off unless QEMU_HOSTMEM_COLLAPSE=1: on a Radeon 890M (RADV, Linux
+     * 6.18) a macOS guest on reims-graphics-pci over collapsed RAM panicked
+     * one to three minutes into every boot with its kernel's memory
+     * overwritten (page tables, locks, ledgers), and ran clean without it.
+     */
+    if (g_strcmp0(g_getenv("QEMU_HOSTMEM_COLLAPSE"), "1") != 0) {
+        return;
+    }
     /* A 2 MiB page is a huge page only over 4 KiB base pages. */
     if (!backend->prealloc || !backend->share ||
         (async && backend->prealloc_context) ||
-- 
